WEBVTT

NOTE Internet Monitor tour: history (en). Generated by tools/tutorial/build.cjs.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.083
The History page keeps every
outage and slowdown, day by day.

2
00:00:04.083 --> 00:00:08.300
Everything is stored on this computer,
and nothing is sent anywhere.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.438
Pick a day here. Today is shown first.

4
00:00:11.438 --> 00:00:15.833
You can also type to search,
for example a date or a weekday.

5
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:20.073
Show filters the list:
all events, only outages,

6
00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:23.433
only unstable periods, or only recoveries.

7
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:29.891
Export day saves the day you picked as a CSV file,
which opens in Excel or Google Sheets.

8
00:00:29.891 --> 00:00:33.567
You can also export the last
7 or 30 days in one file.

9
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:38.013
These four numbers sum up the day:
how many outages there were,

10
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:42.767
the total time offline, how many unstable
periods, and the longest outage.

11
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.305
The table lists every event of the
day with its time, newest first.

12
00:00:47.305 --> 00:00:51.567
Disconnected marks the start of an outage.
Back online marks its end.

13
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:54.609
Duration tells how long each state lasted.

14
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:58.195
On a Disconnected row,
that is how long you were offline.

15
00:00:58.195 --> 00:01:02.033
On a Back online row, it is how
long the line then stayed up.

16
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:07.567
Cause says what failed. TCP ping,
HTTP and DNS are the three kinds of checks.

17
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.967
When all three fail,
the whole connection was down.

18
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.233
When only one fails,
part of the internet stopped

19
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.433
working, for example
websites couldn't be found.

20
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.891
Unstable means the line stayed up,
but lost many packets or became very slow.

21
00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:26.700
Calls break up and pages load
slowly during these periods.

22
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.453
With Outages selected,
only the drops are left, which makes

23
00:01:30.453 --> 00:01:34.033
them easy to count and to compare
with what your provider says.

24
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:37.534
Monthly report opens the
whole month on one page,

25
00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:40.255
ready to print or to send to your provider.

26
00:01:40.255 --> 00:01:41.900
It has its own video tour.

27
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.367
Press How this page works on any page to
watch its video. Thanks for watching!
